# Vectis Mail Server — submission trace-header sanitisation
# Generated by config engine — do not edit manually
#
# Applied ONLY by the submission-cleanup service, which master.cf wires to the
# submission (587) and smtps (465) listeners. Inbound :25 uses the default
# cleanup service and is deliberately untouched — stripping Received there
# would destroy the inbound trace chain and Postfix's hop-count loop detection.
#
# WHY: Postfix stamps the submitting client into Received — its HELO name, rDNS
# name and public IP, plus (because smtpd_tls_received_header = yes) its TLS
# version and cipher. On an authenticated submission that client is the
# TENANT'S OWN application server, so the header discloses their infrastructure
# to every recipient, their mail provider, and anyone the message is forwarded
# to. The client IP remains in the Postfix maillog; this only stops it being
# broadcast to recipients.
#
# DKIM is unaffected: rspamd's signing header list does not include Received
# (verified against the shipped default in lua_ffi/dkim.lua), and cleanup runs
# after the milter, so nothing that is signed is altered here.
#
# `regexp:` (POSIX ERE, built into Postfix) is used deliberately rather than
# `pcre:` — the vectis-postfix image does not ship postfix-pcre, so a pcre map
# would fail to load and take the submission listener down with it.
{{ if .Postfix.SubmissionHeaderStripEnabled }}
# 1. Sanitise, don't delete. Keep the "by <us> (Postfix) with <proto>" hop so
#    the message still carries the trace information RFC 5321 s4.4 requires,
#    and drop everything before it (the client clause and any TLS details).
#    The leading .* is GREEDY on purpose: a hostile HELO containing the literal
#    text "by x (Postfix) with y" would otherwise be selected by a non-greedy
#    match, and the real client clause would survive. Greedy always anchors on
#    the LAST such occurrence, which is the one Postfix itself appended.
/^Received:.*[[:space:]]by[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]]+)[[:space:]]+\(Postfix\)[[:space:]]+with[[:space:]]+([A-Za-z0-9]+)/ REPLACE Received: from [redacted] by $1 (Postfix) with $2

# 2. Fail CLOSED. Any Received that rule 1 did not match — a folded variant we
#    did not anticipate, or one supplied by the client from a non-Postfix MTA —
#    is removed outright rather than passed through. An unmatched header must
#    never fall through to the recipient still carrying an address.
/^Received:/ IGNORE

# 3. X-Originating-IP is a pure network identifier the sender never chose to
#    publish (Roundcube and various app frameworks add it). Always removed.
/^X-Originating-IP:/ IGNORE

# 4. Authentication-Results is stamped by rspamd on the submission path as
#    "Authentication-Results: ORIGINATING; auth=pass smtp.auth=<SASL user> ..."
#    and travels to every recipient. That publishes the tenant's SUBMISSION
#    CREDENTIAL identity — the same class of disclosure as the client clause in
#    rule 1, and worse whenever the SASL username differs from the visible From
#    (a shared or per-tenant relay user, or a mailbox sending as an alias).
#
#    Removed unconditionally rather than matched on "ORIGINATING": a
#    client-supplied Authentication-Results is also unsafe to forward, since a
#    receiver may treat it as its own trusted resolver's verdict. Fail closed,
#    matching rule 2.
#
#    Scoped to submission/smtps only. The INBOUND :25 Authentication-Results is
#    untouched (that cleanup service applies no header_checks) and stays
#    load-bearing for spam scoring and sieve.
/^Authentication-Results:/ IGNORE
{{- range .Postfix.StripSubmissionExtraHeaders }}
# Operator-configured extra header (postfix.strip_submission_extra_headers).
/^{{ . }}:/ IGNORE
{{- end }}
{{- else }}
# strip_submission_client_headers is disabled — no rules are active. The file is
# still rendered and mounted so that toggling the setting needs only a config
# regen, never a compose/mount change (a mount change would require the
# two-step cross-version Apply dance).
{{- end }}
